      id: 'html-01',
      title: 'A page about your favorite place',
      summary: 'Add a heading, a short paragraph, a link, and an image.',
      explanation: [
        "A web page starts with content: words, pictures, and actions. HTML means HyperText Markup Language. “Markup” means adding labels around content so a browser knows what each part means. A browser is the program that opens a website. An HTML element is a whole piece, such as a heading together with its tags. In <p>Hello!</p>, <p> starts a paragraph, Hello! is the content, and </p> ends it. The slash means “this element ends here.” HTML names meaning and structure; colors and spacing are usually CSS's job.",
        'This is a complete small HTML document. Save it as index.html and open it in a browser.\n' +
          '\n' +
          '```html\n' +
          '<!doctype html>\n' +
          '<html lang="en">\n' +
          '  <head>\n' +
          '    <meta charset="utf-8">\n' +
          '    <title>Mountain lookout</title>\n' +
          '  </head>\n' +
          '  <body>\n' +
          '    <h1>Mountain lookout</h1>\n' +
          '    <p>I like watching the clouds drift past.</p>\n' +
          '  </body>\n' +
          '</html>\n' +
          '```\n' +
          '\n' +
          'The first line asks for modern HTML behavior. html wraps the document; lang="en" says its language is English, which helps tools read it aloud. head holds information about the page. title names the browser tab. body holds the visible content. h1 gives the main heading, and p gives the paragraph. meta provides a document setting. charset="utf-8" tells the browser to interpret the file using UTF-8, a character encoding that can represent letters and symbols from many languages.',
        `The angle brackets < and > mark tags. An attribute is an extra setting inside an opening tag. In lang="en", lang is the attribute name and "en" is its value. The equals sign connects them; quotes mark where the value starts and ends. Nesting means putting one element inside another. In the example, h1 and p are inside body. Close the inside element before closing its parent: <body><p>Text</p></body>. Indenting each nested level makes this relationship easier to see. Indentation helps people; it does not choose the tag's meaning.`,
        'An anchor, written a, makes a link. Its href attribute holds the destination address. The visible words between <a> and </a> tell readers what they will open. These jobs are different: changing the words does not change the address. Use useful words such as “Read the park guide,” rather than “Click here.” A full address such as https://example.org/lookout includes a scheme, https, and a site name. A local filename can also be a destination. The destination still needs to exist; making a link does not create the other page.',
        'These two elements can go inside body, after the paragraph in the first example.\n' +
          '\n' +
          '```html\n' +
          '<a href="https://example.org/lookout">\n' +
          '  Read the lookout guide\n' +
          '</a>\n' +
          '<img\n' +
          '  src="lookout.jpg"\n' +
          '  alt="Clouds above a rocky mountain viewpoint"\n' +
          '>\n' +
          '```\n' +
          '\n' +
          "Clicking the link asks the browser to open the href address. src means the image's source: the file or address from which to load it. This example needs a real lookout.jpg file beside index.html. img is a void element, meaning it has no content between opening and closing tags, so it needs no </img>. The attributes still belong to its opening tag even when they use separate lines.",
        `Alt text is a written replacement for an image's useful information. It can help when the picture fails to load or when someone uses a screen reader, a tool that speaks or presents page content in another way. Describe the information that matters here, rather than writing “image” or repeating a filename. A decorative image that adds no information normally gets alt="". This project's image is informative, so give it a useful description. A long description can pass a length check while still being unhelpful; read your own words and ask what a person would learn from them.`,
        'Before the project, predict what happens if you change href but keep the link words: the destination changes while the words stay the same. Now predict changing alt: the image file stays the same, while its written description changes. For the exercise, keep a main heading and add a short p, an a whose href is exactly https://example.org/trail, and an img with descriptive alt text. Check that all four parts exist, then actually follow the link and inspect the picture. The automatic checks can find the elements and address; they cannot judge whether your description matches the real image.'
      ],
      analogy: {
        familiar: 'A small poster with title, note, directions and picture',
        connection: 'Tags label the job of each page part',
        limit: 'A label does not make a link useful unless its destination is correct.'
      },
      steps: [
        'Add one main heading with h1 and a short paragraph with p.',
        'Link to https://example.org/trail with clear link words.',
        'Add an image. Use its alt text to describe what the picture shows.',
