    {
      id: 'html-04',
      title: 'A page usable without a mouse',
      summary: 'Give images useful descriptions and controls clear names. Check every action with the keyboard.',
      explanation: [
        'Accessibility means making content and actions usable by people with different ways of seeing, hearing, moving, and understanding. Someone might use a keyboard, a screen reader, a touch screen, or a large zoom setting. Your HTML should still tell them what is there and what each control does. Start with built-in elements that already have useful behavior. A button is an action control; an a with href is a navigation link. Their meaning helps a browser and assistive tools offer the right interaction.',
        'This is a complete example with an informative image and a named control.\n' +
          '\n' +
          '```html\n' +
          '<!doctype html>\n' +
          '<html lang="en">\n' +
          '  <head>\n' +
          '    <meta charset="utf-8">\n' +
          '    <title>Bike repair day</title>\n' +
          '  </head>\n' +
          '  <body>\n' +
          '    <main>\n' +
          '      <h1>Bike repair day</h1>\n' +
          '      <img\n' +
          '        src="repair.jpg"\n' +
          '        alt="A volunteer helping a child fix a bicycle tire"\n' +
          '      >\n' +
          '      <button type="button">View repair times</button>\n' +
          '    </main>\n' +
          '  </body>\n' +
          '</html>\n' +
          '```\n' +
          '\n' +
          'The image requires a repair.jpg file. alt describes its useful information. The button has visible words that explain its purpose. This HTML creates the control; it does not yet supply code to open repair times.',
        `An image's src tells the browser which picture to load. alt gives a text replacement. Imagine someone cannot see this picture: what information would they miss? Write that information in ordinary words. You usually do not need “picture of,” because the tool can already announce that it is an image. Decorative pictures that add no information normally have alt="". Do not leave alt out and assume that is the same: some tools may announce the file name. img has no closing tag because it is a void element, with no content inside it.`,
        `A control's accessible name is the name assistive tools use to identify it. For an ordinary button, its visible text supplies that name. Clear visible text helps everyone. Prefer “View repair times” to “Go” when the action would otherwise be unclear. type="button" makes it a plain button; inside a form, this avoids accidentally submitting the form. A native button can receive keyboard focus and can be activated using normal keyboard controls. A div with button-like styling does not automatically gain those behaviors.`,
        'This fragment belongs inside body and demonstrates navigation beside an action control.\n' +
          '\n' +
          '```html\n' +
          '<a href="directions.html">Read directions to the workshop</a>\n' +
          '<button type="button">Show workshop checklist</button>\n' +
          '<img src="small-dot.svg" alt="">\n' +
          '```\n' +
          '\n' +
          "The link needs a directions.html page and navigates to it. The button needs behavior added later if it should show a checklist. The small dot is decorative, so its empty alt lets assistive tools skip it. These choices depend on each element's purpose, not on its color or shape. Do not copy empty alt onto a picture that teaches useful information.",
        `Keyboard focus marks the control that will receive a keyboard action. Press Tab to move forward through focusable controls and Shift+Tab to move backward. Check that you can see where focus is. A focused button should be usable with Enter or Space; a link normally activates with Enter. tabindex="-1" removes an element from normal Tab navigation, so it is not a way to make this exercise's button easy to reach. Keep the source order sensible and keep visible focus styling. These are useful checks, but they do not cover every accessibility need.`,
        'For the picnic exercise, add alt text that describes the informative image and give the native button the exact visible name View picnic details. Keep it keyboard focusable. Tab to it, check its name, and inspect focus. The automated checks verify text and basic focusability; the starter does not add the actual details-opening behavior. A button passing the naming check can still do nothing when activated. To judge a finished interactive page, test the real action, review images in context, and use a screen reader as well as the keyboard.'
      ],
      analogy: {
        familiar: 'A door people need to find and open',
        connection: 'Clear names and keyboard focus make controls easier to use',
        limit: 'A keyboard check is not a complete accessibility test.'
      },
      steps: [
        'Describe the picnic image in alt text.',
        'Give the button a name that says what it does.',
        'Keep the button as a button element, not a link or a div.',
        'Press Tab until you reach the button. Check that you can see which button is selected.'
      ],
      starter: '<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>Community picnic</title></head><body><main><h1>Community picnic</h1><img src="picnic.jpg"><!-- TODO: describe image and name button --><button></button></main></body></html>',
      solution: '<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>Community picnic</title></head><body><main><h1>Community picnic</h1><img src="picnic.jpg" alt="Neighbors sharing food at a park picnic table"><button type="button">View picnic details</button></main></body></html>',
      runtime: 'html',
      checks: [
        {
          label: 'Image has a description',
          expression: 'document.querySelector("img")?.alt.trim().length>8'
        },
        {
          label: 'Button has a clear name',
          expression: 'document.querySelector("button")?.textContent.trim()==="View picnic details"'
        },
        {
          label: 'Native button is keyboard focusable',
          expression: 'document.querySelector("button")?.tabIndex>=0'
        }
      ],
      quiz: {
        question: 'What is a useful first keyboard check?',
        choices: [
          'Tab to the button and confirm its name',
          'Change its color',
          'Set tabindex=-1'
        ],
        answer: 0,
        explanation: 'A keyboard user should be able to reach the button and understand its purpose.'
      },
      hints: [
        'Describe what is informative in the image.',
        'A native button can be reached by pressing Tab.',
        'Add nonempty alt and a visible button name.'
      ],
      math: null,
      filename: 'index.html',
      estimatedMinutes: 14,
      externalNotes: 'Automated checks do not prove full screen-reader accessibility.'
    },
    {
      id: 'html-05',
      title: 'An activity signup form',
      summary: 'Add labeled name, email, and activity fields with a submit button.',
      explanation: [
        'A form collects related choices or information. The form element groups its controls. An input is a single entry field; a select offers choices; a button can submit the form. A label tells a person what a control asks for. Controls need meaningful names even before someone types anything. A placeholder inside a field disappears while typing and should not replace its label. HTML can ask the browser to catch simple missing or malformed values, but saving information needs additional behavior and another system to receive it.',
        'This form fragment belongs inside body. It demonstrates a labeled required field.\n' +
          '\n' +
          '```html\n' +
          '<form>\n' +
          '  <label for="visitor">Visitor name</label>\n' +
          '  <input id="visitor" name="visitor" type="text" required>\n' +
          '\n' +
          '  <label for="contact">Contact email</label>\n' +
          '  <input id="contact" name="contact" type="email" required>\n' +
          '\n' +
          '  <button type="submit">Request information</button>\n' +
          '</form>\n' +
          '```\n' +
          '\n' +
          `The label's for value matches the input's id exactly